  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote `src/main.ts`, and it passes `typecheck`. I couldn't run it in a browser or run `vite build`, so I haven't seen a rendered frame.\n\n- **Island:** the terrain is one custom mesh built in code. It is flat-shaded: each triangle has its own vertices and face normal. It sits on a jittered grid, and the face colours (sea floor, sand, grass, forest, rock, snow) come from height and slope. On it are about 24 two-tier low-poly pine trees and about 12 rocks, placed by height and slope and drawn as instanced primitives.\n- **Water:** a large polar-grid mesh with a custom GLSL material. Vertex waves are animated through a `waveClock` uniform, and the facets are flat-shaded from screen-space derivatives. It has a fresnel mix with the sky colour, a sun glint, wave crests, and a fade to the horizon colour at distance.\n- **Day/night:** 20 seconds is one full day, starting at 09:00. Each frame it moves the sun and moon, calls `app.atmosphere.setSky` for the sky colours and stars, and sets the colour and intensity of the sun, moon and hemisphere fill lights. The sun turns orange near the horizon, and the fill light never drops below 0.5 intensity, so the scene stays lit at night. The water material is lit by the same light.\n- **Camera:** it sits 21 m above the water and orbits slowly around the island, which is about 34 m across. If `app.camera` is unavailable, the camera stays fixed at the starting framing.\n- **Other:** it draws into the existing `#app` canvas. `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set after `app.ready()` plus two animation frames. `?hour=N` freezes the clock for captures.\n\n**Risk:** `setLight`, which varies the light intensity and colour, is behind the engine's `A3D_QR_LIGHTING` flag, which I couldn't confirm is on by default. I call it with `?.`, so without it the lights keep their starting values.\n\n",
